morning everyone so one morning to young  fish are swimming along when all of a  sudden an older fish swims by in the  opposite direction the old fish turns to  the young fish and says morning boys  how's the water the two yo fish smile  and nod swim on for a little bit and so  one of them turns to the other and says  what the hell is water so the author  David Foster Wallace told this story to  the graduating class of kenyon college  in 2005 the point of the fish story he  told students is that the most immediate  important realities the ones that shape  our existence that sustain us are often  the ones that are the hardest to see and  to talk about so I thought about this  story a lot as I was applying to the GSB  and trying to go to that question what  matters most to you and why I must have  written a dozen draft of that essay and  each one was more convoluted and more  awful than the one before at one point  when I couldn't answer the question I  turned to my friends and my family  thinking maybe they had the answer  that's right in true Silicon Valley  style I tried to outsource the answer to  it was obviously a very deeply personal  question and then just to make matters a  little bit worse I would go to the  admissions website and I would watch  videos of current students who would  talk about how much they'd love the  application process and in particular  how much they liked answering that  question what matters most to you so  maybe some of you feel that way but I  didn't for me the process was torturous  and the end product it was an  overwrought convoluted 800 words about  culture culture so I felt like I got  into GSB in spite of that essay and  definitely not because of it and so when  I got to GSB last fall I felt like a  little bit of a fraud I felt like I'd  tricked Derek and I also felt I'm  probably only the only one who feels  this way so clearly that's not true  clearly a lot of us have no idea what  matters most to us  and that's crazy right because we're  pretty smart and scrappy Bunch we've got  the answers to a lot of questions and  yet we can't figure out this really  simple question so I've got an idea a  monthly graduation speech and because it  was inspired by this fish story and  thinking about calling it what's water  so here's what I want to do every month  I want to bring an amazing speaker to  the GSB to give a commencement address  and this isn't just any kind of speech  it's a reflective speech in which the  speaker is going to reflect on their own  experiences their job their life  everything holistically and in  reflecting on their experiences they're  going to give us an opportunity to think  our own experience here at the GSB in a  different way so now some of you might  be asking yourselves a very good  question your solution is more speakers  so fair enough right there are a ton of  speakers here at the GSB and in fact in  preparing for this speech I looked up  who's coming over the next week or so  here's the list this is a full time job  right this is about 50 speakers if you  went to all of these speeches this is  all you would do here are the ones that  I'll probably go to either because  they're awesome like Vinod Khosla or  because I worked for them like Jane Chen  or because I find them interesting  because of their industry or whatever  but there are two people I'm really  excited about seeing two people that I'm  almost certainly going to carve out time  for that's these two our classmates gaya  and Jasper we're giving talk next Monday  so talk is awesome right because talk is  different from just about everything we  do at the GSB talk is an opportunity to  sit quietly for an hour try to hold off  on those cell phones and listen to our  classmates tell their stories to talk  about what motivates them what has  challenged them what continues to  challenge them and inevitably is I  listen to talk I think about myself in a  different way I reflect on my own  experiences here and how I might want to  shape them a little bit differently so I  got to thinking what if we could do  something like talk but with famous  people the kind of people that are on  this list the kind of people we invite  to events like view from the top but  instead of spending 55 minutes talking  about the strategic tactical moves  they've made to get to where they are  now in their career we've really honed  in on those five minutes the five  minutes where somebody inevitably asked  a question like if you had one piece of  advice for us what would it be and then  I realized that already exists it's the  graduation speech so now some of you  might be asking yourselves another very  good question don't we already have a  graduation speaker we do but in response  to that question I would tell you this  little story when i graduated from  pomona college i went to Poland for a  year and somewhere around late-september  early October I realized was getting  cold and then I realized was  get really cold and I'm a Californian I  hadn't bothered to pack my winter jacket  so good starving student I did what any  starving student with you I called my  mom and I begged her to send me my  winter jacket so my poor worried mother  she dutifully packaged up my winter  jacket and she sent it off by surface  mail so what my jacket arrived so we're  around mid-april the following year this  is a true story especially if you know  my mother you'll know is true and she I  took that Jack and I stuffed it in the  back of my closet I had no use for it  anymore so I would just ask you do you  really want to wait to the end of your  two years here at the GSB to hear this  kind of talk and do you want just one  shot at an inspirational speech a little  early sorry doesn't ignore that slide so  I was asked you to reflect on the word  commencement to commence means to begin  and in the context of the undergrad  experience a commencement address comes  at the end of the academic experience  but just as undergrads are commencing  their foray into the real world so I  think this makes a lot of sense right  because as an undergrad your experience  with the real world has been pretty  limited probably a couple unpaid  internships but the GSB is different  you're at the GSB we have this weird  real world school hybrid we're aware of  the real world at every moment whether  it be in the speakers that are coming to  campus the jobs that were worried about  the summer internships that we're  applying for we're under no illusions  that the real world is here and we're  constantly running trying to figure out  how to make best use of that sometimes I  feel a little bit like a cartoon  character in the cartoons i watch as a  kid cartoons like this one we're a  little gopher is running for a survival  on a conveyor belt running running  frantically and even as a kid I would  think to myself there's a clear escape  route like just jump off that conveyor  belt  so now as an adult as a GSB student I  like to think I'm smarter than this  cartoon gopher and yet I find myself  doing the same thing running from class  2a BBL to my club activity to a small  group dinner too busy to take a step  back pause and think about what really  matters to me so as I am today I'd like  to ask you for three things as I try to  make the monthly commencement address  what's water a reality here at the GSB  the first is this if you have ideas if  you have connections send them to me  secondly if you're super excited about  this idea and you want to get involved  i'm looking to create a team that would  actually make this happen I can't do it  alone and I don't want to so if you're  excited come and talk with me and  finally for the rest of you I just asked  for you to carve out time an hour a  month to listen to an amazing speaker an  hour a month to jump off that conveyor  belt an hour a month to take a step back  and ask yourself the question what's  water thank you
 

